use std
into ecad.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with ec_ is exported). It is the printed-circuit-board engine of the Electronics workspace: it generates footprint pads, finds which copper is connected, builds the ratsnest of unrouted connections, runs design-rule checks, routes the board on a grid with vias, pours copper zones and produces the drawing operations for Gerber and drill files. Every length is in millimeters on the board plane, x to the right and y up. Layer 0 is the top copper and layer 1 the bottom copper; a layer mask is 1 for top only, 2 for bottom only and 3 for both. Net 0 means a pin with no net.

```amni
extern fn ec_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "ec_view_f64";
extern fn ec_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "ec_view_i32";
```

Module state. Ints, all starting at 0: `NP` (pads), `NT` (tracks), `NV` (vias), `NB` (part bodies), `NN` (nets, so net ids run from 0 to `NN` minus 1), `NI` (copper items), `GX`, `GY`, `GL` (router grid columns, rows and layers), `SG` (search stamp). Growable f64 arrays: `PR` (32 rule parameters), `PD` (10 per pad), `TK` (8 per track segment), `VI` (4 per via), `PB` (6 per part body), `CI` (12 per copper item), `OE` (6 per ratsnest line), `DV` (8 per rule violation), `RT` (8 per routed segment), `RV` (4 per routed via), `RI` (8 router results), `FP` (8 per footprint pad), `ZS` (6 per poured rectangle), `GA` (4 per aperture), `GO` (6 per drawing operation), `DS` (1 per grid node), `KY` (1 per pad of a net). Growable i32 arrays: `NS` (1 per net), `UF` (1 per item), `JN` (1 per item), `OBT`, `OBV`, `PV`, `ST`, `TG`, `TI`, `HP` (1 per grid node each), `HV` (heap of nodes), `PN` (routed path nodes of the current net), `PL` (one path), `MK` (1 per pour cell), `QQ` (pour queue), `LR`, `CR` (3 per strip), `PJ` (pad items of a net), `IN` (1 per pad of a net), `PA` (1 per pad of a net), `OR` (net order).

`PR` holds: 0 clearance, 1 track width, 2 via diameter, 3 via drill, 4 grid step, 5 board left, 6 board bottom, 7 board right, 8 board top, 9 board-edge clearance, 10 via cost, 11 copper layers (1 or 2), 12 smallest track width, 13 smallest drill, 14 solder-mask expansion, 15 smallest annular ring, 16 silkscreen line width, 17 board-edge line width.

A pad in `PD` is x, y, width, height, shape (0 rectangle, 1 round or oval), net, layer mask, drill (0 for a surface pad), part index, pin number. A track segment in `TK` is x0, y0, x1, y1, width, layer, net, unused. A via in `VI` is x, y, net, unused; every via has the `PR` via diameter and drill and spans both layers. A part body in `PB` is left, bottom, right, top, height, side (0 top, 1 bottom). A poured rectangle in `ZS` is left, bottom, right, top, net, layer.

A copper item in `CI` is: 0 kind (0 an axis-aligned box, 1 a capsule: every point within radius r of a segment), 1 layer mask, 2 net, 3 x0, 4 y0, 5 x1, 6 y1 (the box's lower-left and upper-right corners, or the capsule segment's two ends), 7 r, 8 source (1 pad, 2 track, 3 via, 4 poured rectangle), 9 source index, 10 cluster (the union-find root after `ec_rats`), 11 unused.

Sizing helpers. `zf(xs, n, x)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries equal to x. `zi(xs, n, x)` takes an i32 growable array by reference and does the same, resizing to n entries equal to x as i32. `rs(xs, n)` and `ri(xs, n)` take an f64 or i32 growable array by reference: xs is emptied and then gets `reserve`(n). Every array in this file is sized only through these four helpers; no block defines other sizing helpers.

The exported function `ec_views()` registers every array JavaScript reads or writes: f64 views 1 `PR`, 2 `PD`, 3 `TK`, 4 `VI`, 5 `PB`, 7 `OE`, 8 `DV`, 9 `RT`, 10 `RV`, 11 `RI`, 12 `FP`, 13 `ZS`, 14 `GA`, 15 `GO`, 16 `CI` through ec_view_f64, and i32 view 17 `NS` through ec_view_i32.

The exported function `ec_setup(np, nt, nv, nb, nn)` takes five i32 counts and stores them in `NP`, `NT`, `NV`, `NB` and `NN`; with `zf` it sizes `PR` to 32 zeros, `PD` to 10·np zeros, `TK` to 8·nt zeros, `VI` to 4·nv zeros, `PB` to 6·nb zeros and `RI` to 8 zeros; `NS` gets `zi` of nn zeros; `CI` gets `rs` of 12 times (np plus nt plus nv plus 70000), `ZS` gets `rs` of 6 times 65536, `OE` gets `rs` of 6 times 8192, `DV` gets `rs` of 8 times 8192, `RT` gets `rs` of 8 times 16384, `RV` gets `rs` of 4 times 4096, `FP` gets `rs` of 8 times 1024, `GA` gets `rs` of 4 times 1024 and `GO` gets `rs` of 6 times 262144; `UF` and `JN` get `ri` of np plus nt plus nv plus 70000; then it calls `ec_views`.

The exported function `ec_zclear()` empties `ZS` keeping its reserve, calls `ec_views` and returns 0.

Geometry helpers on reals. `ptseg(px, py, ax, ay, bx, by)` is the distance from point P to the segment AB: with d equal to B minus A and L2 its squared length, t is 0 when L2 is below 1e-18 and otherwise ((P minus A) dot d) over L2 clamped to 0 through 1; the result is the length of P minus (A plus t·d). `ptbox(px, py, x0, y0, x1, y1)` is the distance from P to the rectangle with those corners: dx is the larger of x0 minus px, 0 and px minus x1, dy likewise with y, and the result is the square root of dx squared plus dy squared. `cross3(ax, ay, bx, by, cx, cy)` is (bx minus ax)·(cy minus ay) minus (by minus ay)·(cx minus ax). `segx(ax, ay, bx, by, cx, cy, dx, dy)` returns 1 when segments AB and CD intersect and 0 otherwise: with d1 `cross3`(C, D, A), d2 `cross3`(C, D, B), d3 `cross3`(A, B, C), d4 `cross3`(A, B, D), they intersect when d1·d2 is at most 0 and d3·d4 is at most 0 and not all four are zero; when all four are zero (collinear) they intersect when the x ranges overlap and the y ranges overlap (each range from the smaller to the larger end). `segseg(ax, ay, bx, by, cx, cy, dx, dy)` is 0 when `segx` is 1, and otherwise the smallest of `ptseg`(A, C, D), `ptseg`(B, C, D), `ptseg`(C, A, B) and `ptseg`(D, A, B). `segbox(ax, ay, bx, by, x0, y0, x1, y1)` is 0 when A or B lies inside the rectangle (x0 at most x at most x1 and y0 at most y at most y1) or when `segx` is 1 for AB and any of the four rectangle sides (x0,y0 to x1,y0; x1,y0 to x1,y1; x1,y1 to x0,y1; x0,y1 to x0,y0); otherwise it is the smallest of `ptbox` of A, `ptbox` of B and `ptseg` of each of the four rectangle corners to AB.

Item helpers. For an item index i its record starts at 12·i in `CI`. `ilay(i, l)` returns 1 when item i is on layer l (0 or 1): its mask is 3, or the mask is 1 and l is 0, or the mask is 2 and l is 1; otherwise 0. `ishare(i, j)` returns 1 when the masks of items i and j are equal or either is 3, otherwise 0. `pdist(px, py, i)` is the distance from point P to item i: for a box `ptbox` of P and its corners, for a capsule `ptseg` of P and its segment minus r. `idist(i, j)` is the gap between items i and j: when both are boxes, dx is the larger of 0 and (the larger of the two x0) minus (the smaller of the two x1), dy likewise with y, and the gap is the square root of dx squared plus dy squared; when one is a box and the other a capsule, `segbox` of the capsule segment and the box minus the capsule r; when both are capsules, `segseg` of the two segments minus both radii. `bx0(i)`, `by0(i)`, `bx1(i)`, `by1(i)` give item i's bounding rectangle: for a box its corners, for a capsule the smaller (for bx0, by0) or larger (for bx1, by1) end coordinate minus or plus r. `inear(i, j, e)` returns 1 when the bounding rectangles of i and j grown by e on every side overlap, otherwise 0.

`additem(kind, mask, net, x0, y0, x1, y1, r, src, idx)` appends those ten values, then 0 (cluster) and 0 to `CI` and adds 1 to `NI`.

The exported function `ec_items()` returns an i32. It empties `CI` keeping its reserve and sets `NI` to 0. For every pad p (record at 10·p in `PD`) with x, y, w, h, shape, net and mask: when the shape is 0, or w equals h, or the shape is 1 and the pad is square, it adds a box (kind 0) from (x minus w/2, y minus h/2) to (x plus w/2, y plus h/2) with r 0 when the shape is 0, and a capsule from (x, y) to (x, y) with r equal to w/2 when the shape is 1 and w equals h; an oval (shape 1 with w different from h) becomes a capsule: when w is larger, from (x minus (w minus h)/2, y) to (x plus (w minus h)/2, y) with r h/2, otherwise from (x, y minus (h minus w)/2) to (x, y plus (h minus w)/2) with r w/2; source 1 and index p. For every track t in `TK` it adds a capsule from (x0, y0) to (x1, y1) with r width/2, mask 1 when its layer is 0 and 2 otherwise, its net, source 2, index t. For every via v it adds a capsule from (x, y) to (x, y) with r equal to `PR`[2]/2, mask 3, its net, source 3, index v. For every poured rectangle z in `ZS` (count is the length of `ZS` over 6) it adds a box with its corners, mask 1 when its layer is 0 and 2 otherwise, its net, source 4, index z. It calls `ec_views` and returns `NI`.

Union-find. `ufind(i)` follows `UF` from i to the root (an entry equal to its own index) and then sets `UF` of every item on that walk to the root, returning the root. `uunion(i, j)` finds both roots and, when they differ, sets `UF` of the larger root to the smaller root.

`connect()` sizes `UF` with `zi` to `NI` entries and sets every entry to its own index. For every pair of items i below j that have the same net, the net above 0, `ishare` 1, `inear`(i, j, 1e-6) 1 and `idist`(i, j) at most 1e-6, it calls `uunion`(i, j). Then it writes `ufind` of every item into its cluster field (`CI` entry 10).

`ratsnet(k)` adds the ratsnest lines of net k. `PJ` is emptied (keeping its reserve after `ri` of `NI` on the first use when its capacity is below `NI`) and gets every item with source 1 and net k. With m the count of `PJ`, when m is below 2 nothing happens. `IN` gets `zi` of m zeros, `PA` `zi` of m entries minus 1 and `KY` `zf` of m entries 1e30. A pad's point is the center of its item (the middle of x0 and x1, the middle of y0 and y1). Start: every pad whose cluster equals the cluster of pad 0 gets `IN` 1, and for each such pad a the keys of all other pads b not in the tree become the smaller of their key and the distance between the points of a and b, with `PA` of b set to a when the key improved. Then, repeatedly: pick the pad q not in the tree with the smallest key (stop when none is left); with c its cluster, append to `OE` the point of `PA`[q], the point of q, the net k and the key (the line length); then every pad with cluster c joins the tree, and for each joining pad a the keys of all pads b still outside the tree become the smaller of their key and the distance from a to b (setting `PA` of b to a when it improved).

The exported function `ec_rats()` returns an i32. It calls `connect`, empties `OE` keeping its reserve, calls `ratsnet`(k) for every net k from 1 to `NN` minus 1, calls `ec_views` and returns the length of `OE` over 6.

`viol(kind, x, y, a, b, v, na, nb)` appends kind, x, y, a, b, v, na, nb to `DV` when the length of `DV` is below 8 times 8192.

The exported function `ec_drc()` returns an i32. It empties `DV` keeping its reserve. With c the clearance `PR`[0]: for every pair of items i below j where `ishare` is 1, `inear`(i, j, c) is 1, and the nets differ or either net is 0, with g equal to `idist`(i, j): when g is at most 1e-9 it calls `viol` with kind 2 (short), and otherwise when g is below c minus 1e-6 with kind 1 (clearance); the location is the middle of the two items' bounding-rectangle centers, a and b are i and j, v is g, na and nb their nets. For every track whose width is below `PR`[12] minus 1e-9, `viol` kind 3 at the middle of the track with a the track's item index, b minus 1, v the width. For every item whose source is not 4, with e equal to the smallest of `bx0` minus `PR`[5], `by0` minus `PR`[6], `PR`[7] minus `bx1` and `PR`[8] minus `by1`, when e is below `PR`[9] minus 1e-6, `viol` kind 4 at the bounding-rectangle center with v equal to e. For every pad with a drill above 0: when the drill is below `PR`[13] minus 1e-9, `viol` kind 6 at the pad with v the drill; when (the smaller of its width and height minus its drill) over 2 is below `PR`[15] minus 1e-9, `viol` kind 7 with v that ring. When `NV` is above 0 and `PR`[3] is below `PR`[13] minus 1e-9, `viol` kind 6 at the first via with v `PR`[3]. Then it calls `ec_rats` and for every ratsnest line calls `viol` kind 5 at the middle of the line with a and b minus 1, v its length, na and nb its net. It calls `ec_views` and returns the length of `DV` over 8.

Footprints. `fpad(x, y, w, h, shape, drill, pin, mask)` appends those eight values to `FP`. The exported function `ec_fp(kind, n, pitch, pw, ph, span, drill, ep)` takes kind and n as i32 and the rest as reals, empties `FP` keeping its reserve, writes the pads of one footprint centered as described, calls `ec_views` and returns the pad count (the length of `FP` over 8). A surface pad has shape 0, drill 0 and mask 1. A through-hole pad has drill `drill`, mask 3, size pw by pw, shape 0 for pin 1 and shape 1 for every other pin. Kind 1, a two-terminal chip: pin 1 at (minus span/2, 0) and pin 2 at (span/2, 0), size pw by ph. Kind 2, SOT-23: pin 1 at (minus pitch, minus span/2), pin 2 at (pitch, minus span/2), pin 3 at (0, span/2), size pw by ph. Kind 3, a dual-row surface package with m equal to n div 2: pin i for i from 1 to m at (minus span/2, (m minus 1)/2·pitch minus (i minus 1)·pitch), and pin m plus i at (span/2, minus (m minus 1)/2·pitch plus (i minus 1)·pitch), size pw by ph. Kind 4, a quad package with m equal to n div 4 and o equal to (m minus 1)/2·pitch: for i from 1 to m, pin i at (minus span/2, o minus (i minus 1)·pitch) size pw by ph; pin m plus i at (minus o plus (i minus 1)·pitch, minus span/2) size ph by pw; pin 2m plus i at (span/2, minus o plus (i minus 1)·pitch) size pw by ph; pin 3m plus i at (o minus (i minus 1)·pitch, span/2) size ph by pw; when ep is above 0, pin n plus 1 at (0, 0) of size ep by ep. Kind 5, a dual-row through-hole package (DIP): the positions of kind 3 with through-hole pads. Kind 6, a single-row header: pin i for i from 1 to n at (0, minus (i minus 1)·pitch), through-hole. Kind 7, a double-row header with m equal to n div 2: for k from 1 to m, pin 2k minus 1 at (0, minus (k minus 1)·pitch) and pin 2k at (pitch, minus (k minus 1)·pitch), through-hole. Kind 8, SOT-23-5: pins 1, 2 and 3 at (minus pitch, minus span/2), (0, minus span/2) and (pitch, minus span/2), pin 4 at (pitch, span/2) and pin 5 at (minus pitch, span/2), size pw by ph. Any other kind writes no pads.

Router grid. With g equal to `PR`[4], a grid node (i, j, l) has index (l·`GY` plus j)·`GX` plus i and its cell center is x equal to `PR`[5] plus (i plus 0.5)·g, y equal to `PR`[6] plus (j plus 0.5)·g. `owner(o, k)` returns k plus 1 when k is above 0 and minus 1 otherwise. `paint(which, kind, x0, y0, x1, y1, r, mask, net, t)` marks every node near a shape: the shape is a box from (x0, y0) to (x1, y1) when kind is 0 and otherwise the capsule of that segment with radius r; for the layers l from 0 to `GL` minus 1 that the mask covers (mask 3 both, 1 layer 0, 2 layer 1), and for the columns i from the floor of ((smaller x minus r minus t minus `PR`[5]) over g minus 0.5) to the ceiling of ((larger x plus r plus t minus `PR`[5]) over g minus 0.5) and the rows j likewise with y and `PR`[6], each clamped to the grid, with d the distance from the cell center to the shape (`ptbox` for a box, `ptseg` minus r for a capsule): when d is below t, the node's entry in `OBT` (which 0) or `OBV` (which 1) becomes the owner value when it was 0, stays when it already equals the owner value, and becomes minus 1 otherwise; the owner value is `owner`(0, net).

`grid()` sets g from `PR`[4], `GX` to the ceiling of (`PR`[7] minus `PR`[5]) over g, `GY` to the ceiling of (`PR`[8] minus `PR`[6]) over g (each at least 1) and `GL` to 2 when `PR`[11] is at least 2 and 1 otherwise; with n equal to `GX`·`GY`·`GL` nodes, `OBT`, `OBV`, `PV`, `ST`, `TG`, `TI` and `HP` get `zi` of n entries (0 for all except `PV` and `HP`, which get minus 1), `DS` gets `zf` of n entries 1e30 and `HV` gets `ri` of n plus 16.

`obstacles()` fills `OBT` and `OBV`. With c the clearance, w the track width `PR`[1] and vd the via diameter `PR`[2]: every node whose cell center lies closer than `PR`[9] plus w/2 to any board edge (x minus `PR`[5], `PR`[7] minus x, y minus `PR`[6], `PR`[8] minus y) gets `OBT` minus 1, and closer than `PR`[9] plus vd/2 gets `OBV` minus 1. Then every item calls `paint` into `OBT` with t equal to c plus w/2 and into `OBV` with t equal to c plus vd/2, using its kind, corners or ends, r, mask and net.

Indexed min-heap on `HV` with `HP` holding each node's slot (minus 1 when not in the heap) and keys in `DS`. `hswap(a, b)` swaps heap slots a and b and updates `HP` of both nodes. `hup(s)` moves slot s up while its key is below its parent's key (parent (s minus 1) div 2). `hdown(s)` moves slot s down to the smaller child (2s plus 1 and 2s plus 2, within the heap length) while that child's key is below its key. `hpush(v, d)` (node v, cost d): when `HP` of v is minus 1 it appends v to `HV`, sets `HP` of v to the last slot and sets `DS` of v to d, then `hup` of that slot; otherwise, when d is below `DS` of v, it sets `DS` of v to d and calls `hup` of `HP` of v. `hpop()` returns the node in slot 0: it swaps slot 0 with the last slot, removes the last slot (`HP` of the removed node becomes minus 1) and calls `hdown`(0) when the heap is not empty.

`touch(v)` prepares node v for the current search: when `ST` of v differs from `SG` it sets `ST` of v to `SG`, `DS` of v to 1e30, `PV` of v to minus 1 and `HP` of v to minus 1.

`cellsof(i, l, mode)` visits the nodes of layer l under item i: every node whose column and row lie within the item's bounding rectangle (columns from the floor of (`bx0`(i) minus `PR`[5]) over g to the floor of (`bx1`(i) minus `PR`[5]) over g, rows likewise, clamped to the grid) and whose cell center has `pdist` at most 0 from the item, plus always the node containing the item's bounding-rectangle center (clamped). For mode 0 each such node gets `touch` and `hpush` with cost 0; for mode 1 each gets `TG` equal to `SG` and `TI` equal to i.

`okt(v, k)` is 1 when `OBT` of v is 0 or k plus 1, or `TG` of v equals `SG`; otherwise 0. `okv(c, k)` (c a column-row index j·`GX` plus i) is 1 when for every layer l below `GL` the node c plus l·`GX`·`GY` has `OBV` 0 or k plus 1, with no exception for target nodes so a via always keeps its clearance; otherwise 0.

`search(k)` runs one shortest-path search for net k and returns the reached target node or minus 1. `HV` is emptied. Sources: for every item of net k with `JN` 1, for each layer l below `GL` that the item is on, `cellsof`(item, l, 0); and every node in `PN` gets `touch` and `hpush` with cost 0. Targets: for every item of net k with `JN` 0, for each layer l below `GL` that it is on, `cellsof`(item, l, 1). Then while `HV` is not empty: v is `hpop`; when `TG` of v equals `SG` it returns v; with l, j, i the node's layer, row and column and d its `DS`: for each of the eight neighbors (di, dj) with di and dj in minus 1, 0, 1 and not both 0, inside the grid, node u on the same layer: when `okt`(u, k) is 1, and for a diagonal move also `okt` of the two nodes (i plus di, j) and (i, j plus dj) on that layer are 1, the cost is d plus g (straight) or d plus g times 1.41421356 (diagonal); after `touch`(u), when that cost is below `DS` of u, `PV` of u becomes v and `hpush`(u, cost). When `GL` is 2 and `okv` of the cell is 1, the node u on the other layer of the same cell gets the same treatment with cost d plus `PR`[10]. When the heap empties it returns minus 1.

`emit(t, k)` turns the path ending at node t into copper for net k. `PL` is emptied and gets t, then `PV` of t, and so on until minus 1, then is reversed (source first). Walking `PL` from the start, a run starts at the first node; for each next node: when its layer differs from the current node's layer, the current run (from its start node to the current node) is emitted as a segment when the two nodes differ, a via is emitted at the current node's cell center, and the run restarts at the next node; otherwise when the step direction (column and row difference) differs from the previous step direction on this run, the run up to the current node is emitted and a new run starts at the current node. At the end the last run is emitted when its start differs from the last node. Emitting a segment appends to `RT` the start and end cell centers, the width `PR`[1], the layer, k and 0, adds its length to `RI`[4], adds 1 to `RI`[2] and calls `paint` for `OBT` with t equal to `PR`[0] plus `PR`[1]/2 and for `OBV` with t equal to `PR`[0] plus `PR`[2]/2, using a capsule of that segment with r `PR`[1]/2, mask 1 for layer 0 and 2 for layer 1, and net k. Emitting a via appends x, y, k, 0 to `RV`, adds 1 to `RI`[3] and calls `paint` for both maps the same way with a capsule from the point to itself, r `PR`[2]/2 and mask 3. Every node of `PL` is appended to `PN`.

The exported function `ec_route()` returns an i32. It calls `ec_rats` (clusters and ratsnest), empties `RT` and `RV` keeping their reserves, sizes `RI` to 8 zeros, calls `grid` and `obstacles`, and gives `PN` and `PL` `ri` of `GX`·`GY`·`GL` and `OR` `ri` of `NN`. The net order: every net k from 1 to `NN` minus 1 with `NS` of k equal to 0 and at least one ratsnest line, sorted ascending by the sum of its ratsnest line lengths (insertion sort into `OR`). `JN` gets `zi` of `NI` zeros. For each net k in that order: `PN` is emptied; r0 is the cluster of the first item of net k with source 1, and every item of net k whose cluster is r0 gets `JN` 1. Then repeatedly: when no item of net k with source 1 has `JN` 0, the net is done; `SG` increases by 1 and t is `search`(k); when t is minus 1, `RI`[1] increases by the number of distinct clusters among the net's pad items with `JN` 0 and the net is done; otherwise `emit`(t, k) runs, `RI`[0] increases by 1, and every item of net k whose cluster equals the cluster of item `TI` of t gets `JN` 1. At the end it calls `ec_views` and returns `RI`[1] as an i32.

Copper pour. The exported function `ec_pour(net, layer)` takes two i32 and returns the number of rectangles it appended to `ZS`. It calls `grid` (only for `GX` and `GY`), and `MK` gets `zi` of `GX`·`GY` zeros (cells j·`GX` plus i). With c the clearance, g the grid step and m equal to c plus 0.71·g: every cell whose center lies closer than `PR`[9] plus 0.71·g to any board edge gets `MK` 1; every item on that layer (`ilay`) whose net differs from net, or whose net is 0, marks with `MK` 1 every cell in its bounding rectangle grown by m (clamped) whose center has `pdist` below m. `QQ` gets `ri` of `GX`·`GY`; every item on that layer with this net (above 0) visits the cells in its bounding rectangle whose centers have `pdist` at most 0, plus the cell containing its bounding-rectangle center, and every such cell with `MK` 0 gets `MK` 2 and is appended to `QQ`. A breadth-first flood over `QQ` (a read index walking forward) gives every 4-neighbor cell with `MK` 0 the value 2 and appends it. Then rows j from 0 to `GY` minus 1, with `LR` holding the previous row's strips and `CR` the current row's (3 values each: rectangle index in `ZS`, first column, last column; both get `ri` of 3·`GX` plus 3 before the first row): each maximal run of columns i0 through i1 with `MK` 2 looks for a strip in `LR` with the same i0 and i1; when found, that rectangle's top (`ZS` entry 6·index plus 3) becomes `PR`[6] plus (j plus 1)·g and the strip is appended to `CR` unchanged; otherwise a new rectangle (`PR`[5] plus i0·g, `PR`[6] plus j·g, `PR`[5] plus (i1 plus 1)·g, `PR`[6] plus (j plus 1)·g, net, layer) is appended to `ZS` when its length is below 6 times 65536, and the strip with its index is appended to `CR`. After each row `LR` becomes a copy of `CR` and `CR` is emptied. It calls `ec_views` and returns the number of rectangles appended.

Gerber and drill operations. `ap(t, w, h)` returns the index of the aperture in `GA` (4 values: type, w, h, 0; type 0 circle of diameter w, 1 rectangle, 2 obround) whose type equals t and whose w and h are within 1e-9, appending a new one when none matches. `op(o, a, x, y, x2, y2)` appends those six values to `GO`. Operation codes: 1 flash at (x, y), 2 a stroke from (x, y) to (x2, y2), 3 a filled rectangle with corners (x, y) and (x2, y2), 4 a drill hit at (x, y) with tool a. `padap(p, e)` returns the aperture of pad p grown by e on every side: with w and h its size plus 2e, `ap`(1, w, h) for shape 0, `ap`(0, w, w) for shape 1 when w equals h, and `ap`(2, w, h) otherwise.

The exported function `ec_gerber(layer)` takes an i32 layer code, empties `GA` and `GO` keeping their reserves, writes the operations and returns the number of operations (the length of `GO` over 6) after calling `ec_views`. Codes 1 and 2 are top and bottom copper with mask bit b (1 for code 1, 2 for code 2) and layer index code minus 1: every pad whose mask is 3 or equals b flashes `padap`(p, 0) at its center; every via flashes `ap`(0, `PR`[2], `PR`[2]); every track on that layer strokes with `ap`(0, its width, its width); every poured rectangle on that layer is a filled rectangle with aperture 0 after `ap`(0, 0.1, 0.1). Codes 3 and 4 are the top and bottom solder mask with b 1 or 2: every pad on b flashes `padap`(p, `PR`[14]). Codes 5 and 6 are the top and bottom paste with b 1 or 2: every pad on b with drill 0 flashes `padap`(p, 0). Code 7 is the board outline: with a equal to `ap`(0, `PR`[17], `PR`[17]), four strokes from (`PR`[5], `PR`[6]) to (`PR`[7], `PR`[6]), then to (`PR`[7], `PR`[8]), then to (`PR`[5], `PR`[8]), then back to (`PR`[5], `PR`[6]). Codes 8 and 9 are the top and bottom silkscreen with side 0 or 1: with a equal to `ap`(0, `PR`[16], `PR`[16]), every part body on that side strokes its four rectangle sides. Code 10 is the drill file: every pad with a drill above 0 gets a drill hit with tool `ap`(0, drill, drill) and every via a drill hit with tool `ap`(0, `PR`[3], `PR`[3]).

`main()` does nothing.
